    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d294">

                <pb facs="0377" n="279" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg279"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/388: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland (Wilson) to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-09-28T22:00:00+01:00">September 28, 1932—10
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 28—6:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>10. Yen has written Secretary General letter dated 26th which refers to Council’s
                    decision concerning Lytton report and requests convocation of Assembly Committee
                    of Nineteen to consider the duration of the extension of 6 months’ period in
                    accordance with resolution of July 1st.<anchor n="80" xml:id="d294-f1"/> Also
                    insists that Committee of Nineteen take steps to prevent Japan from aggravating
                    the situation in the meantime.</p>
                <p>Drummond says they will summon Committee of Nineteen for a public session on
                        Friday<anchor n="81" xml:id="d294-f2"/> as the request seems reasonable. The
                    Committee will probably explain to Yen why a definite duration cannot now be
                    fixed but he does not know what line will be taken on preventing Japan from
                    aggravating the situation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
